WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.200
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.340
Today you are going to witness how an AI learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:13.680
to perform these amazing juggling&nbsp;
skills. And not only with the ball.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:20.760
And I am very excited for this. Why is&nbsp;
that? Well, because I see that this paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:26.580
was co-authored by our friend, Sebastian&nbsp;
Starke, which means that an amazing paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:33.960
is almost guaranteed. He was behind this&nbsp;
work too, and many other amazing papers.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:41.880
Now, creating a little AI that can learn these&nbsp;
kinds of skills seamlessly is immensely difficult.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:49.620
Why? Well, the two hallmarks of a great animation&nbsp;
system is that the transitions between movements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:56.280
are great and that no foot sliding happens.&nbsp;
And, as you see, this previous technique is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:03.720
unfortunately not it. Yet, you see this kind of&nbsp;
animation in a lot of computer games, even today.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:11.820
And, unfortunately, it gets even worse. Look!&nbsp;
The ball does not obey the laws of physics. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:17.820
it seems that getting quality animations&nbsp;
for gaming is absolutely hopeless. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:21.300
is that it? Well, don’t despair quite yet!

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:27.120
Now, previous techniques required a lot of&nbsp;
manual labor in creating these animations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:35.100
and weaving these animations together. However,&nbsp;
today, we see more and more AI-based systems that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:42.480
can have a look at a big soup of movement data&nbsp;
that shows how humans move. Why is that? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:49.440
this is a learning based system, so it has a bit&nbsp;
of an understanding of what is happening. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:56.520
what does that give us? Well, hopefully exactly&nbsp;
what we are looking for! It promises a smooth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:04.500
transition from any move into any other move.&nbsp;
Well, after seeing these previous results, I don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:11.160
have very high hopes, so I will believe it when I&nbsp;
see it. This is how a previous technique was able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:18.660
to animate this character. Not great. And now,&nbsp;
hold on to your papers, and let’s see the new one!

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:27.960
Oh wow. Just look at that! These animations are&nbsp;
indeed fluid, and no matter how we combine these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:34.620
movements together, the AI is able to weave&nbsp;
them together into one smooth and believable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:42.120
motion. It makes up these beautiful transitions&nbsp;
on the spot without ever having seen them. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:48.660
this captured my imagination. So what do&nbsp;
experienced Fellow Scholars like us do here?

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:55.680
Of course, we make it harder. Much&nbsp;
harder. How? Well, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:02.340
let’s add wind to the mixture, constantly&nbsp;
blowing the ball away from the player. Wow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:08.640
that is unbelievable. Not a problem&nbsp;
at all for this new AI. It can adjust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:14.580
to new situations, which is a hallmark&nbsp;
of an intelligent algorithm. Love it.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:20.520
But wait! So what is the extent of new&nbsp;
situations that it can generalize to?

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:28.200
Let’s see. Oh, I love that. Turning motions&nbsp;
are also possible. It had witnessed people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:34.920
turning around in the training data, and it&nbsp;
had seen people juggle. That is not a surprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:40.380
but what is surprising is that it was&nbsp;
able to put the two together seamlessly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:47.340
so it can juggle while turning. That is&nbsp;
absolutely amazing. Now these results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:54.660
are already very impressive, but let’s be a&nbsp;
little cheeky. So, can we ask for even more?

00:03:54.660 --> 00:04:01.440
Well, you know what? No one said that we are&nbsp;
only allowed to juggle a ball. Who said that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:09.180
it has to be a ball? It could be a box.&nbsp;
And my goodness! Look! It still works.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:17.280
And it also works with an American football.&nbsp;
Or even, yes, you are seeing correctly. It even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:25.320
works with toilet paper. So, who is better,&nbsp;
the AI or Lionel Messi? Well, let’s see.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:35.280
Look at that! Haha! Joke’s on you, little&nbsp;
AI! Team Humanity vs Team Robot - 1:0. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:42.840
unfortunately it gets even worse. I am not&nbsp;
sure if I should be showing this to you, so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:48.120
are you prepared? Are you sure? I think&nbsp;
you are not prepared at all for this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:57.000
so if you continue the video, it’s on you.&nbsp;
You’ve been warned. Okay…go. Ouch. Whoo!

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:03.840
This reminds me of an earlier paper where they&nbsp;
wrote up a musculoskeletal simulation system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:10.740
and hamstrung it in a variety of different&nbsp;
ways. That is, these poor little folks got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:16.620
injuries and they simulated how they&nbsp;
would move afterwards. But worry not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:21.720
